25. February 29 in a leap year `29-02-k3f7a2-2024` → accepted.
26. day 31 in a 30-day month `31-04-...` → calendar-invalid.
27. year not four digits: `13-10-k3f7a2-26` → rejected.
28. year five digits `13-10-k3f7a2-20266` → rejected.
29. year `0000` → shape-valid, calendar-invalid.
30. year with letters `13-10-k3f7a2-20a6` → rejected.
31. separators missing/extra/wrong: `1310-k3f7a2-2026`, `13-10-k3f7a2-2026-`, `13_10_k3f7a2_2026`, `13/10/k3f7a2/2026`, `13 10 k3f7a2 2026` → rejected.
32. id has leading/trailing whitespace → likely rejected unless trimmed; spec must decide.
33. id is empty or missing → rejected.
34. id is exactly the literal form with an extra field (e.g., includes slug) → rejected.
35. total length wrong despite right fields → rejected.
36. correct shape with all three hyphens only → accepted.
37. Unicode homoglyph hyphen (en dash) `13–10–k3f7a2–2026` → rejected.
38. non-breaking space between fields → rejected.
39. id with trailing newline in header value → depends on trim; flag.
40. id `13-10-k3f7a2-2026 ` and filename id identical after trim → accepted if trimmed; if not, mismatch flagged.

Date vs write date:
41. id date equals the day the entry file was created → accepted.
42. id date equals commit date but file mtime differs (checkout) → spec must say which date is authoritative.
43. id date is after the file's creation date → broken (future-dated).
44. id date is before file's creation date by one day → broken or timezone edge; flag.
45. entry written 23:59 local but id uses UTC date making it next day → ambiguous; resolve by a stated timezone.
46. entry written just after local midnight, id uses previous local date → broken by the chosen rule.
47. entry written 1 Jan 00:00, id year is the prior year → broken unless timezone says so.
48. entry written across a leap-second/clock change → id date should follow the calendar date.
49. id date matches header's recorded date but not filesystem date → depends on authority.
50. id date matches header date and both differ from git author date → flag.
51. Existing entry from years ago with old-format date but valid shape → accepted (form is era-independent) or rejected if a different era format is expected; flag.

Uniqueness:
52. one entry only → accepted.
53. two entries, same id, different slug, same folder → fail (shared id).
54. two entries, same id, same slug (identical filenames impossible) → fail; git sees a conflict/overwrite.
55. same id in two different subfolders of the memory folder → fail if the checker's scope is the whole folder tree; flag if scope is per-folder.
56. same id in filename but different header id → the filename pair collides; fail.
57. same header id but different filename ids → header pair collides; fail.
58. ids differing only in the random part → distinct, accepted.
59. ids differing only in date → distinct, accepted (not sequential).
60. two entries created on different branches with different random parts → accepted after merge.
61. two branches independently generate the same date and same random part → after merge, two files with the same id → fail.
62. two branches generate the same full id and the same slug → merge conflict; fail whichever resolution keeps duplicates.
63. one branch's id equals another's after a rebase → fail if both survive.
64. duplicate id introduced in an unmerged branch only → not visible in the current tree; checker on the branch should fail; checker on main passes.
65. duplicate present in history but one file later deleted → current tree has one; "no two entries share an id" currently met; historical duplication flagged or not per spec.
66. an id reused after its original file was deleted → currently unique; historical reuse flagged or not per spec.
67. duplicate id where one occurrence is a copy left by `git cp` → fail.
68. duplicate id in a backup file (`foo-<id>-slug.md.bak`) → fail if the checker scans it, otherwise skipped by extension filter.
69. duplicate id between an entry file and a non-entry file that happens to contain the pattern → depends on scope; flag.
70. duplicate detection with case-folding: `K3F7A2` is invalid anyway; `k3f7a2` vs `k3f7a2` identical → fail.
71. duplicate detection with Unicode normalization: visually identical but different bytes → form rejects non-ASCII; ASCII is stable.
72. duplicate id where one file is a symlink to the other → one logical entry or two? flag; likely fail/count once.
73. duplicate id across a cherry-pick where the file exists with a different slug → fail.
74. duplicate id where the two files have different prefixes → still fail.
75. three or more entries sharing one id → fail, grouped.
76. duplicate id among malformed ids (both malformed) → both form failures; uniqueness may be moot.
77. duplicate id where one is valid and one malformed (e.g., uppercase) → form failure plus a near-collision; report both.

Never changes / renames:
78. rename with id unchanged, only slug edited → not one of the three listed failures; but it breaks "the file keeps its name" — flag the tension.
79. rename with id unchanged, only prefix changed → same tension.
80. rename to a different valid id → fail.
81. rename to a malformed id → fail.
82. rename where git reports R100 with the id changed in both filename and header → fail.
83. rename where git reports R with id changed only in the filename, header still old → fail; header/filename now also disagree.
84. rename where git reports R with id changed only in the header, filename still old → id changed; fail.
85. change id in place (no rename), same filename, header id edited → id changed; fail if history is inspected.
86. git reports D + A rather than R (similarity below threshold) with a different id → still a rename to a different id; fail if detected by history/header.
87. rename A→B→A over two commits → net filename id unchanged; a head-only check passes, a history check sees two changes; spec must say which.
88. revert commit restoring the old id after a rename → id temporarily changed; head check passes.
89. squash merge that collapses rename commits → history may show only the final name; head check passes.
90. rebase/force-push rewriting the rename → same.
91. rename staged but not committed → filesystem shows the new name, git index shows rename; checker's behavior depends on whether it reads the tree or the index.
92. working-tree rename not staged → git shows delete + untracked; checker reading the filesystem sees only the new file.
93. rename of the file into a subfolder with the id unchanged → not an id change; accepted by the listed rule.
94. rename across directories with a different id → fail.
95. copy plus delete of the original → git may show R; different id → fail.
96. copy without deleting the original, new id → a second entry; if the id is new and unique, accepted; if the copy keeps the old id, duplicate → fail.
97. case-only rename on a case-insensitive filesystem that changes the id's letters → uppercase invalid → form failure.
98. shallow clone with no history → rename check unavailable; checker must report "cannot check", not pass.
99. folder not under git at all → rename check unavailable; report/skip.
100. git rename detection disabled or threshold changed → checker must not rely solely on `git status`.
101. merge commit that renames a file as a conflict resolution → treated like any rename.
102. filter-repo/filter-branch rewriting the id → fail if the current/historical id differs from the original.
103. symlink retarget from one entry to another → id change by indirection; flag.
104. file deleted and re-added with the same id → id unchanged; accepted.
105. file deleted and re-added with a different id → rename-like id change; fail if history is checked, pass if only the current name is checked.
106. an entry file renamed to a different id but git records it as a delete of one file and add of another with the same content → detected only by content/history.

Filename/header/parsing (context):
107. filename `<prefix>-<id>-<slug>.md` with a valid id, header id identical → accepted.
108. filename valid, header id missing → fail (id absent from header).
109. filename valid, header id malformed → fail.
110. filename malformed, header valid → fail.
111. filename id and header id both valid but different → fail (two ids for one entry / header doesn't match name).
112. filename missing the prefix → naming failure; flag scope.
113. filename missing the slug → naming failure.
114. slug empty → naming failure.
115. slug contains hyphens → accepted; the parser must use the fixed id shape, not hyphen-splitting.
116. slug contains a substring matching an id → parser must anchor on the real id; no false positive.
117. prefix itself contains digits and hyphens → parser must not misidentify a prefix fragment as the id.
118. prefix is empty → filename begins with `-<id>-...`; flag.
119. slug contains uppercase/spaces/non-ASCII → naming issue outside the id rule; flag.
120. extension `.md` vs `.MD` vs `.markdown` → checker may skip non-`.md`; flag.
121. file is not Markdown but sits in the folder → skipped by extension filter or flagged.
122. hidden/editor files (`.foo.md`, `foo.md~`, `.foo.md.swp`, `.#foo.md`) → should be skipped, else false positives.
123. file nested in a subdirectory → scanned or not per the checker's recursion rule.
124. empty memory folder → no violations.
125. folder contains only directories → no violations.
126. a directory named like an entry file → must be ignored.
127. symlinked entry file → followed or not; flag.
128. file is unreadable (permissions) → checker reports an error, not a silent pass.
129. header id appears in YAML front matter `id: <id>` → accepted.
130. header id appears as `**id:** <id>` or in an HTML comment or table → checker's parser coverage; flag if unsupported.
131. header id is quoted (`id: "13-10-k3f7a2-2026"`) → accepted if unquoted/trimmed.
132. header id has a trailing comment (`id: 13-10-k3f7a2-2026 # note`) → accepted if the parser strips the comment, else false failure.
133. header has two `id:` fields, same value → ambiguous duplicate field; flag.
134. header has two `id:` fields, different values → fail.
135. header field name is `ID:`/`Id:` → accepted if case-insensitive, else missed.
136. the id string also appears in body text (an example or a reference) → the checker must read only the header, else false positive.
137. header id has CRLF line endings → accepted if trimmed.
138. file starts with a UTF-8 BOM before the header → checker must tolerate it.
139. header id has leading/trailing spaces or tabs → accepted if trimmed, else mismatch.
140. id split across lines → not found; fail.
141. header id written with backticks (`id: \`13-10-k3f7a2-2026\``) → parser must strip markup.
142. header id in a nested list/item rather than front matter → parser scope; flag.
143. file has no header at all → fail.
144. header is malformed/truncated → fail with a parse error.
145. header id is valid but the date part disagrees with the file's own recorded creation date in the body → flag.

Checker behavior:
146. one violation of form → nonzero exit and the file named.
147. one duplicate → nonzero exit and both files named.
148. one rename → nonzero exit and the old/new ids named.
149. zero violations → exit zero with a clean report.
150. running the checker twice on the same folder → same result (idempotent).
151. checker output names the violated rule and the offending file(s) → verifiable.
152. checker run on a folder with one unreadable file → reports the error and still checks the rest.
153. checker run while a file is being written → may read a partial header; should retry or report rather than false-pass.
154. checker on a very large folder → completes or reports a limit; no silent truncation.
155. checker on a folder not under git → skips the rename rule with a stated reason.
156. checker in a shallow clone → skips the rename rule with a stated reason.
157. checker on an unborn branch (no commits) → rename rule skipped.
158. checker with rename detection disabled by config → must not silently pass renames.
159. checker's regex is anchored to the whole id → no partial-match false positives.
160. checker's regex is unanchored → a 7-char random part could be accepted as 6; false negative; fail this test.
161. checker's regex allows uppercase or wrong lengths → should fail this test.
162. checker lowercases or case-folds before comparing → uppercase would wrongly pass; fail.
163. checker trims whitespace before validating → whitespace-padded ids pass; spec must decide.
164. checker applies Unicode normalization → non-ASCII lookalikes could pass; fail unless the alphabet is enforced.
165. checker's date validation uses the checker's timezone → an id valid in the writer's timezone may fail; flag.
166. checker's date validation is disabled → shape-valid impossible dates (`32-13-...`) pass; if calendar validity is required, fail this test.
167. checker's locale affects lowercase mapping (e.g., Turkish `I`) → only if uppercase appears; flag.
168. checker orders findings: one file with a malformed id and a duplicate → both reported, not one overwriting the other.
169. checker groups duplicates so every member is listed → verifiable.
170. checker reports a mismatch between filename id and header id → verifiable.
171. checker reports `git` rename only when both blobs are entries → no false positives from other files.
172. checker does not treat a slug/prefix rename as an id change → unless the id bytes changed.
173. checker does not treat a delete+add with the same id as a rename-to-different-id.
174. checker's "renamed" detection compares ids, not paths → survives folder moves.
175. checker with paths containing spaces or unicode → still resolves files.
176. checker counts a symlink twice → duplicate false positive; flag.
177. checker follows `.git` internals or ignored files → must not scan them.
178. checker reads `.gitignore`d files → flag.
179. checker on Windows path separators/line endings → same findings.
180. checker with a filename longer than the OS limit → error reported.

Generation/randomness (not directly checkable):
181. random part drawn from `[a-z0-9]` uniformly → accepted.
182. random part generated with a biased or narrow alphabet → form-valid; quality not checkable by the checker.
183. random part generated as a counter (`000001`, `000002`) → form-valid; violates the "never the next free number / random" intent; not detectable by shape alone; flag as a policy finding.
184. random part generated from the machine hostname or timestamp → predictable; form-valid; not shape-detectable.
185. random part generated uppercase then lowercased → could collapse distinct draws into the same string (rare); check collisions.
186. two draws from the same seed on two branches → same random part on the same date → collision; fail.
187. random part truncated by an off-by-one → 5 or 7 chars; fail form.
188. retry after a collision generates a new random part → the second entry gets a different id; accepted.
189. a writer manually edits an id to "fix" a typo → changes id; fail.
190. a writer picks the "next free number" after seeing other entries → id shape may still pass; intent violated; flag.
191. id copied from another entry as a template and not re-randomized → duplicate; fail.
192. the random part includes chars that are easy to transcribe wrongly (`0/o`, `1/l`) → form-valid; usability risk.
193. the id is generated once and reused for a second entry created later (same date) → duplicate; fail.
194. two entries written on the same day with independent random parts → both accepted.
195. an entry written on a different day has a different date prefix → accepted.

Multi-branch/merge/tooling:
196. two branches edit the same entry file, one changes the id → merge; fail by id change.
197. two branches rename the same file to two different ids → duplicates/none for one; fail.
198. a branch deletes an entry, another keeps it → no duplicate; id retired.
199. a branch adds an entry with an id that main later also used → merge collision; fail.
200. a branch moves the memory folder itself → paths change, ids unchanged; accepted; checker's root must be pointed at the new path.
201. CRLF/`.gitattributes` normalization altering header bytes → header parse must survive; else false failure.
202. Git LFS/smudge not applied → header missing on checkout; fail with a reason.
203. sparse checkout excludes some entries → the checker sees a subset; duplicates may be hidden; flag.
204. submodule memory folder → git history checks must run in the submodule.
205. worktree with the same branch checked out twice → checker run twice on the same ids; no new violation.

Also requirement-statement boundary scenarios — maybe include:
206. the id has the right shape but the date is in the future relative to "now" → flag/reject per rule.
207. the id's date is a valid past date but not the day the entry was written → only detectable with an authoritative timestamp; flag.
208. the id uses a two-digit year by mistake in an otherwise valid filename → form failure.
209. the id appears twice in the header but the file has one name → see 133/134.
210. W1.1 form evolves (e.g., allows a 7th char) → old ids remain valid under grandfathering or not; flag.

