20. month `00` (`13-00-...`) → same gap.
21. month `13` (`13-13-...`) → same gap.
22. day `32` (`32-10-...`) → same gap.
23. `30-02-...` → same gap.
24. `29-02-...` in a non-leap year (`2023`) → same gap.
25. `29-02-k3f7a2-2024` → passes if calendar validity is required and leap years are handled.
26. day `31` in a 30-day month (`31-04-...`) → same gap.
27. year not four digits (`-26`, `-20266`) → fails.
28. year containing a letter (`-20a6`) → fails.
29. year `0000` → in shape, calendar-invalid; spec gap.
30. separators removed (`1310-k3f7a2-2026`) → fails.
31. separators changed to `/`, `.`, `_` or space → fails.
32. extra or trailing separator (`13-10-k3f7a2-2026-`) → fails.
33. Unicode dash/en-dash instead of `-` → fails.
34. non-breaking space between fields → fails.
35. leading or trailing whitespace in the filename id → fails unless the checker trims; spec must state trimming.
36. leading or trailing whitespace in the header id value → passes if trimmed; otherwise flagged as a mismatch.
37. id is empty or the field is missing in filename and/or header → fails.
38. id total length is wrong even though fields look right (e.g., a six-char field under a five-char regex) → fails.
39. id correct in all fields → passes.
40. filename id valid, header id identical → passes.
41. filename id valid, header id missing → fails: the entry's id is not in the header.
42. filename id valid, header id malformed → fails.
43. filename id malformed, header id valid → fails.
44. filename id and header id both valid but different → fails: one entry carries two ids / name and header disagree.
45. filename id and header id differ only by case or whitespace → fails or is normalized, per the trimming/case rule.
46. id date matches the day the entry file was written → passes.
47. id date is one day after the write date → fails the "day it is written" clause if an authoritative timestamp exists.
48. id date is one day before the write date → fails on the same clause; timezone ambiguity must be resolved.
49. entry written at 23:59 local where UTC is the next day and the id uses UTC → spec gap: which clock is authoritative.
50. entry written just after local midnight with the previous day's id → fails under a local-date rule.
51. entry written on 1 January whose id carries the previous year → fails under a local-date rule.
52. id date matches the header's recorded date but not the filesystem/git date → spec gap: which date the checker may trust.
53. id date is in the future relative to the checker's clock → fails as not the write date, unless clocks/granularity explain it.
54. very old entry with a valid-shape id from a previous format era → passes on form; may fail if W1.1 is applied retroactively — needs a grandfathering rule.
55. one entry in the folder → passes.
56. two entries with the same id and different slugs → fails: shared id.
57. two entries with the same id where both filenames would be identical → fails; git also cannot hold both names.
58. two entries with the same id in different subfolders of the memory folder → fails if the checker's scope is the whole tree; passes if scope is a single folder — spec gap.
59. two entries whose filenames share an id but whose header ids differ → fails: shared filename id.
60. two entries whose headers share an id but whose filename ids differ → fails: shared header id.
61. two entries whose ids differ only in the random part → passes: distinct ids.
62. two entries whose ids differ only in the date part → passes: distinct ids, and neither is "the next free number".
63. two branches generate different random parts on the same date → passes after merge.
64. two branches generate the same date and the same six random characters → fails after merge: collision.
65. two branches generate the same full id with the same slug → fails: filename collision at merge.
66. a rebase reproduces one branch's id on top of the other's — same as 64/65.
67. duplicate id exists only on an unmerged branch → checker on that branch fails; checker on main passes; the rule's scope over branches must be stated.
68. duplicate id existed in history but one file was later deleted → current tree has one id; whether historical sharing counts as "two entries share an id" is a spec gap.
69. an id is reused after its original entry is deleted → currently unique; whether reuse is forbidden is a spec gap.
70. a copied file left beside the original keeps the same id → fails: duplicate.
71. a backup or temp file in the folder contains a second copy of the id → fails if scanned, ignored if the checker filters extensions — spec must state the filter.
72. duplicate id where one file is a symlink to the other → one logical entry or two? spec gap; likely counts once.
73. duplicate id where the two files have different prefixes → fails: prefix does not make the id distinct.
74. three or more entries sharing one id → fails; the report must list every member.
75. duplicate id where both occurrences are malformed → both fail on form; the duplicate is secondary.
76. duplicate id where one is valid and one is malformed (e.g., uppercase) → both reported: the malformed one on form, the pair on near-collision.
77. rename with the id unchanged and only the slug edited → not one of the three named failures, but it breaks "the file keeps its name" — spec gap: is this a violation?
78. rename with the id unchanged and only the prefix changed → same gap.
79. file renamed to a different valid id → fails.
80. file renamed to a malformed id → fails on rename-to-different-id and on form.
81. id changed in both the filename and the header, git shows R100 → fails.
82. id changed in the filename only, header keeps the old id → fails on rename and on filename/header mismatch.
83. id changed in the header only, filename keeps the old id → fails: the entry's id changed.
84. id edited in place with no rename (same filename, header changed) → fails the "an id never changes" clause.
85. git shows delete + add (similarity below the rename threshold) with a different id → fails if the checker reads history/content, not only `git status`.
86. rename A→B→A over two commits → head shows the original name; a head-only check passes and a history check fails — spec gap: which is authoritative.
87. revert commit restoring the old id after a rename → id changed in between; same gap as 86.
88. squash merge collapsing the rename commits → history may show only the final name; the check must not depend on commit granularity.
89. rebase or force-push rewriting the rename → same as 88.
90. rename staged but not committed → git index shows R, working tree shows the new name; checker behavior depends on whether it reads the index or the tree — spec must state.
91. rename in the working tree, not staged → git shows delete + untracked; a filesystem-only checker sees only the new file.
92. entry moved to another folder with the id unchanged → passes: not an id change.
93. entry moved to another folder with a different id → fails.
94. copy of an entry with a new id, original kept → a second, distinct entry; passes if the new id is unique and well-formed.
95. copy of an entry with the old id, original kept → fails: duplicate.
96. case-only rename on a case-insensitive filesystem → uppercase makes the id fail form.
97. shallow clone with no history → the rename rule cannot be evaluated; expected "not checkable", not a silent pass.
98. folder not under git → rename rule not checkable; must be reported as skipped.
99. git rename detection disabled or threshold changed → the checker must compare ids itself rather than trust `git status`.
100. merge conflict resolution renames a file → treated as any rename.
101. history rewritten by filter-repo/filter-branch → a changed id must still be compared with the original.
102. symlink retargeted from one entry to another → an id change by indirection; spec gap.
103. file deleted and re-added with the same id → passes: the id did not change.
104. file deleted and re-added with a different id → fails if history is checked; a head-only check passes — same gap as 86.
105. entry renamed to a different id but git records it as a same-content delete/add → only content/history comparison catches it.
106. filename is `<prefix>-<id>-<slug>.md` and the header id matches → passes.
107. filename missing the prefix → outside the id rule; naming violation, must be reported if naming is in scope.
108. filename missing the slug or with an empty slug → same.
109. slug contains hyphens → passes; the parser must key on the fixed id shape rather than splitting on hyphens.
110. slug contains a substring that itself looks like an id → passes; the parser must anchor on the real id and not report a phantom id.
111. prefix contains digits and hyphens that look like date/random fields → passes; the parser must not misidentify a prefix fragment as the id.
112. prefix is empty so the filename begins with `-<id>-<slug>.md` → naming gap.
113. slug contains uppercase, spaces or non-ASCII → outside the id rule; naming gap.
114. extension `.MD` or `.markdown` → the checker's extension filter decides; must be stated, not silently skipped.
115. non-Markdown file placed in the folder → skipped by the filter or flagged; must be stated.
116. editor or hidden files (`.foo.md`, `foo.md~`, `.foo.md.swp`, `.#foo.md`) → must be ignored to avoid false positives.
117. entry nested in a subdirectory → scanned or not, per the recursion rule; must be stated.
118. empty memory folder → no violations.
119. folder contains only directories → no violations.
120. a directory whose name matches the entry-file pattern → must be ignored.
121. entry file is a symlink → followed or not; must be stated, and following can create a duplicate false positive.
122. entry file is unreadable → the checker reports an error for it and still checks the rest.
123. header id in YAML front matter (`id: <id>`) → passes.
124. header id as inline bold, an HTML comment, or a table cell → passes only if the parser supports it; otherwise the checker misses a real id.
125. header id quoted (`id: "13-10-k3f7a2-2026"`) → passes if quotes are stripped.
126. header id with a trailing comment (`id: <id> # note`) → passes if the comment is stripped, else a false violation.
127. two `id:` fields with the same value → ambiguous header; must be flagged as duplicate field or accepted; spec gap.
128. two `id:` fields with different values → fails.
129. field name written `ID:` or `Id:` → passes only if the parser is case-insensitive; otherwise missed.
130. the id string also appears in the body as an example or a cross-reference → must not count; the checker reads only the header.
131. header uses CRLF line endings → must parse the same as LF.
132. file begins with a UTF-8 BOM → must parse the same as without.
133. header id has leading/trailing spaces or tabs → trim rule must be stated.
134. the id value is split across two lines → not found; fails.
135. the id is wrapped in backticks or markdown emphasis → passes only if markup is stripped.
136. header is absent entirely → fails.
137. header is truncated or malformed → fails with a parse error, not a silent pass.
138. header id valid but the body's stated creation date disagrees with the id date → spec gap: which is authoritative.
139. one form violation → the checker exits nonzero and names the file and rule.
140. one duplicate → exits nonzero and names both files.
141. one rename-to-different-id → exits nonzero and names the old and new ids.
142. no violations → exits zero with a clean report.
143. the checker is run twice on the same folder → identical result (idempotent).
144. output must name the violated rule and every offending file to be verifiable.
145. one unreadable file among many → that file's error is reported and the others are still checked.
146. a file is being written during the scan → partial header; the checker retries or reports, never a false pass.
147. a very large folder → completes or reports a stated limit; no silent truncation.
148. a folder not under git → the rename rule is skipped with a stated reason.
149. a shallow clone → the rename rule is skipped with a stated reason.
150. an unborn branch / no commits → the rename rule is skipped with a stated reason.
151. `git` rename detection disabled by config → the checker must not silently pass.
152. the checker's regex is anchored to the full id → no partial-match false positives.
153. the checker's regex is unanchored → a 7-character random part can be accepted as 6; a false negative.
154. the checker's regex allows uppercase or the wrong lengths → false negatives; this test must fail the checker.
155. the checker case-folds before comparing → an uppercase id wrongly passes; this test must fail the checker.
156. the checker trims whitespace before validating → a padded id passes; the trim rule decides.
157. the checker normalizes Unicode → a lookalike id passes; only safe if the [a-z0-9] alphabet is enforced after normalization.
158. the checker's date validation uses a different timezone than the writer → a valid id may be flagged; must be pinned.
159. the checker does no calendar validation → `32-13-...` passes; fails this test if calendar validity is required.
160. the checker's locale changes letter casing (e.g., Turkish `I`) → only matters where uppercase appears; test with a locale set.
161. one file has both a malformed id and a duplicate → both findings are reported; neither hides the other.
162. duplicates are grouped so every member is listed → required for verifiability.
163. a filename/header id mismatch is reported as its own finding → required.
164. git rename detection is restricted to entry files → other renames in the repo do not create false positives.
165. a prefix or slug rename is not reported as an id change because the id bytes are unchanged → required.
166. a delete+add with the same id is not reported as a rename-to-different-id → required.
167. rename comparison is on ids, not paths → a folder move with the same id passes.
168. paths with spaces or non-ASCII → files still resolve and are checked.
169. the checker follows `.git` internals or ignored files → must not; otherwise false positives.
170. Windows path separators and CRLF → the same findings as on Linux.
171. a filename longer than the OS limit or otherwise unreadable → an error is reported.
172. random part drawn uniformly from `[a-z0-9]` → passes.
173. random part from a biased or narrow alphabet → form-valid; the checker cannot judge randomness.
174. random part generated as a counter (`000001`, `000002`) → form-valid but violates "not the next free number"; only detectable by policy review, not by the checker.
175. random part derived from hostname or timestamp → predictable; form-valid, not shape-detectable.
176. random part generated uppercase then lowercased → collisions possible after folding; uniqueness check catches them.
177. two branches seed the same RNG identically → same random part on the same date → collision; fails.
178. random part truncated by an off-by-one → 5 or 7 characters; fails form.
179. a collision is retried with a fresh random part → the new entry's id is unique; passes.
180. a writer hand-edits an existing id to "fix" it → the id changed; fails.
181. a writer chooses the next free number after reading other entries → may pass form while violating the intent; flag.
182. a writer copies another entry as a template and forgets to re-randomize → duplicate; fails.
183. the id was generated once and reused for a second entry on the same date → duplicate; fails.
184. two entries written on the same day with independent random parts → both pass.
185. an entry written on a different day carries a different date prefix → passes; the date prefix is not a sequence.
186. two branches edit the same entry, one changing the id → fails on the id change.
187. two branches rename the same entry to two different ids → at least one id is lost; fails.
188. one branch deletes an entry and another keeps it → no duplicate; the id is simply retired.
189. a branch adds an id that main later also used → merge collision; fails.
190. the memory folder itself is moved in a branch → ids unchanged; passes, and the checker must be pointed at the new root.
191. `.gitattributes` line-ending normalization changes header bytes → parsing must survive; otherwise false failure.
192. Git LFS/smudge leaves the header unexpanded on checkout → the checker reports a parse failure, not a pass.
193. sparse checkout omits some entries → duplicates may be hidden; the checker must state its scope.
194. the memory folder is a submodule → history checks must run inside the submodule.
195. two worktrees check out the same branch → running the checker in both finds no new violation.
196. W1.1 is revised to a longer random part → old ids either keep passing under a grandfather rule or start failing; must be stated.
197. the id uses a two-digit year elsewhere in the name (not the id) → the id itself is unaffected; only the id fields are checked.
198. the id appears in a link or anchor in another file → not an entry id; the checker reads entry files only.
199. a file with a valid id has zero bytes of body → passes; the rule constrains the id, not the content.
200. the entry is written but not yet committed → the checker on the working tree passes; git-based rename checks have no commit to compare.

